WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:03.086
(host)
You spot a goblin crouched
like a cat lying in wait.

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:04.170
Just when you least expect it,

00:00:04.254 --> 00:00:05.797
you're on his back
and he's taking you

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:08.466
on what will surely
be the wildest

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:11.386
and most terrifying
ride of your life.

00:00:11.469 --> 00:00:13.638
The Puca,
a shape-shifting goblin,

00:00:13.722 --> 00:00:16.766
terrorizes his victims at
night and plays cruel jokes.

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.351
He carries unwilling riders

00:00:18.435 --> 00:00:20.603
through brambles
and narrow cliffs

00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:22.897
and bestows magical gifts.

00:00:22.981 --> 00:00:25.316
Existing somewhere between
boogeyman, scapegoat,

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:27.068
and temperamental fay,

00:00:27.152 --> 00:00:28.695
the known trickster
animal spirit

00:00:28.778 --> 00:00:31.573
was once dreaded across
Ireland, the Channel Islands,

00:00:31.656 --> 00:00:34.951
and England, and has
survived the test of time.

00:00:35.034 --> 00:00:37.662
Don't believe me?
Just ask Shakespeare.

00:00:37.746 --> 00:00:39.330
[triumphant music]

00:00:39.414 --> 00:00:43.585
I'm Dr. Emily Zarka,
and this is "Monstrum."

00:00:43.668 --> 00:00:45.003
This monster's influence

00:00:45.086 --> 00:00:48.298
is demonstrated by
its hotly-debated etymology.

00:00:48.381 --> 00:00:50.759
Puca means goblin
in Old English,

00:00:50.842 --> 00:00:53.428
but did that word come
from Scandinavian,

00:00:53.511 --> 00:00:55.722
Germanic, or Celtic origins?

00:00:55.805 --> 00:00:58.433
Some scholars trace it
back to Icelandic roots.

00:00:58.516 --> 00:01:00.602
Others say Norwegian origins

00:01:00.685 --> 00:01:03.646
and others, old
Danish or old Swedish.

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:05.064
And there's arguments to be made

00:01:05.148 --> 00:01:08.443
for Irish, Welsh, and the
Cornish etymology as well.

00:01:08.526 --> 00:01:10.862
That's a lot of
colloquial contention.

00:01:10.945 --> 00:01:12.572
The commonality is
that in every language,

00:01:12.655 --> 00:01:16.743
the term refers to a negative
spirit, a mischievous monster.

00:01:16.826 --> 00:01:19.245
Because the
definition is the same

00:01:19.329 --> 00:01:21.372
across so many
different languages,

00:01:21.456 --> 00:01:23.875
yet no one clear origin,

00:01:23.958 --> 00:01:26.628
to me, that suggests that
this monster was around

00:01:26.711 --> 00:01:27.921
for a very long time

00:01:28.004 --> 00:01:30.006
before spreading
across Northern Europe.

00:01:30.089 --> 00:01:32.091
Like a lot of monsters,
the name might differ

00:01:32.175 --> 00:01:33.593
depending on who
is telling the tale

00:01:33.676 --> 00:01:35.094
and where they're telling it,

00:01:35.178 --> 00:01:37.972
but the basic characteristics
are consistent.

00:01:38.056 --> 00:01:39.974
In expressly animal spirit,

00:01:40.058 --> 00:01:42.310
they threaten death
but do not kill.

00:01:42.393 --> 00:01:45.438
They can bestow kindness,
but generally prefer cruelty,

00:01:45.522 --> 00:01:47.816
and they're encountered at night
and during the harvest time

00:01:47.899 --> 00:01:50.026
where they frolic
through the hay.

00:01:50.109 --> 00:01:51.653
While these
shape-shifting tricksters

00:01:51.736 --> 00:01:53.780
can choose to take
on any animal form,

00:01:53.863 --> 00:01:57.325
they favor cows, dogs,
donkeys, goats, and horses,

00:01:57.408 --> 00:01:59.911
particularly
a small shaggy pony.

00:01:59.994 --> 00:02:01.621
In some regions,
they even take on

00:02:01.704 --> 00:02:03.915
a diminutive humanoid form.

00:02:03.998 --> 00:02:05.458
Their otherworldly nature

00:02:05.542 --> 00:02:07.502
is indicated by
completely black coloring

00:02:07.585 --> 00:02:11.214
or animal features
like ears, horns, or tail.

00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:12.423
The Puca seems to exist

00:02:12.507 --> 00:02:14.676
mainly to harass
late-night travelers,

00:02:14.759 --> 00:02:16.678
spooking them out of their wits.

00:02:16.761 --> 00:02:18.304
Their favorite way to do this,

00:02:18.388 --> 00:02:20.598
unexpected rides
through the countryside.

00:02:20.682 --> 00:02:23.017
Yep. The monster lies in wait

00:02:23.101 --> 00:02:25.478
before popping up, tossing
their victim onto their backs

00:02:25.562 --> 00:02:27.647
and bolting across the landscape

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:30.775
with little thought for the
safety or comfort of the rider.

00:02:30.859 --> 00:02:32.527
They intentionally run
through thorny bushes,

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:36.573
alongside stone walls, and
on the precipice of cliffs.

00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:38.533
These death-defying
rides rarely result

00:02:38.616 --> 00:02:42.579
in serious harm to the rider
other than the pure terror.

00:02:42.662 --> 00:02:44.455
In some instances,
the monster takes pity

00:02:44.539 --> 00:02:47.834
on the naive traveler and
might happily trot them home,

00:02:47.917 --> 00:02:50.378
but it is also known
to spoil wild fruits

00:02:50.461 --> 00:02:52.505
by urinating or
spitting on them.

00:02:52.589 --> 00:02:55.008
In one story, the Puca
gives a magical flute

00:02:55.091 --> 00:02:57.218
to a piper that
makes him famous,

00:02:57.302 --> 00:03:00.471
but only after playing a few
cruel jokes on him first.

00:03:00.555 --> 00:03:01.723
According to legend,
this is the same flute

00:03:01.806 --> 00:03:04.851
that makes people
dance until they die,

00:03:04.934 --> 00:03:08.313
which seems like a questionable
gift to say the least.

00:03:08.396 --> 00:03:11.524
Another interesting trait
of this monster, he talks.

00:03:11.608 --> 00:03:13.109
According to the tale,

00:03:13.192 --> 00:03:15.904
a farmhand while putting
away the cows after milking

00:03:15.987 --> 00:03:19.198
finds himself tossed onto
the back of a young horse.

00:03:19.282 --> 00:03:21.242
Throughout the night, the
horse takes him on a rough,

00:03:21.326 --> 00:03:24.203
harrowing ride across
the countryside.

00:03:24.287 --> 00:03:25.872
The next morning, quite
bruised and worse for wear,

00:03:25.955 --> 00:03:28.625
he tells his boss
of the encounter.

00:03:28.708 --> 00:03:31.419
The man's advice, "Wear spurs."

00:03:31.502 --> 00:03:32.754
When the horse appears again,

00:03:32.837 --> 00:03:35.214
the young man asked
to ride on its back,

00:03:35.298 --> 00:03:38.384
but the cunning creature seems
to sense something is afoot.

00:03:38.468 --> 00:03:41.220
It asks, "Have you
the sharp things on?"

00:03:41.304 --> 00:03:44.557
When the farmhand confirms
he does, the horse retreats.

00:03:44.641 --> 00:03:45.683
But this is not the last

00:03:45.767 --> 00:03:47.518
the farmhand will
see of the monster.

00:03:47.602 --> 00:03:48.937
Returning from
a party one night,

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:50.647
the man is startled when a horse

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:53.066
jumps off the path
and knocks him down,

00:03:53.149 --> 00:03:56.444
covering the man with his
body in a protective stance.

00:03:56.527 --> 00:04:00.239
A bright light blasts down on
the path at that same moment.

00:04:00.323 --> 00:04:01.699
The creature rises,

00:04:01.783 --> 00:04:04.702
stating that it saved him
from being killed by a spirit.

00:04:04.786 --> 00:04:06.829
When the farmhand
asks who saved him,

00:04:06.913 --> 00:04:09.749
the horse replies,
"I am the Puca."

00:04:09.832 --> 00:04:12.669
There are many variations
of this Puca tale.

00:04:12.752 --> 00:04:14.796
The bright light may be
interpreted as a demonic force,

00:04:14.879 --> 00:04:17.006
but more commonly,
the spirit or spirits

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:21.427
the story is referring to are
the good people, the fay folk.

00:04:21.511 --> 00:04:24.847
In every version of this
legend, and there are a lot,

00:04:24.931 --> 00:04:28.559
the Puca never calls
the spurs by their name.

00:04:28.643 --> 00:04:31.187
The monster refers to them
only as sharp things

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:33.606
regardless of if the story
is told in English,

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:36.359
Gaelic, or any other language.

00:04:36.442 --> 00:04:38.069
The Puca's aversion
is important enough

00:04:38.152 --> 00:04:39.487
that even in one story

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:41.406
where the creature
attacks a fisherman,

00:04:41.489 --> 00:04:42.949
the man is able
to defeat the Puca

00:04:43.032 --> 00:04:45.660
by attaching a row of hooks
on his heels like spurs.

00:04:45.743 --> 00:04:48.287
Spurs are used to
control horses,

00:04:48.371 --> 00:04:49.956
so why would
a supernatural being

00:04:50.039 --> 00:04:52.375
have such a hatred
of the sharp things?

00:04:52.458 --> 00:04:55.670
Well, spurs are traditionally
made from steel or iron.

00:04:55.753 --> 00:04:57.505
Those of you with a little
knowledge of the fay

00:04:57.588 --> 00:05:00.466
might recognize the folkloric
significance of iron,

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:02.969
which is said to
deter or injure them.

00:05:03.052 --> 00:05:05.930
The Puca's reluctance to
even speak the word for spur

00:05:06.014 --> 00:05:07.682
might be a nod to this tradition

00:05:07.765 --> 00:05:10.727
and could hint at the
monster's own lineage.

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:13.187
The Puca's connection to the
good people of Celtic lore

00:05:13.271 --> 00:05:15.898
reiterates that its
origins are quite old.

00:05:15.982 --> 00:05:17.150
While monks who came to Ireland

00:05:17.233 --> 00:05:18.609
started writing down
these traditions

00:05:18.693 --> 00:05:22.447
around the 17th century, be
assured that oral storytelling

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:26.075
was an integral part of Irish
culture long before that.

00:05:26.159 --> 00:05:29.203
And remember, these stories
are not memorized scripts,

00:05:29.287 --> 00:05:32.540
but ever-changing performances
between Seanachie,

00:05:32.623 --> 00:05:35.585
traditional Gaelic
storytellers, and audience.

00:05:35.668 --> 00:05:37.837
As Christianity's
influence grew in Ireland,

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:40.965
the animal trickster spirit is
replaced by a guardian angel

00:05:41.049 --> 00:05:43.926
or other angelic being in
the farmhand narrative,

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:45.178
turning the story into one

00:05:45.261 --> 00:05:47.138
about the divine
influence of God

00:05:47.221 --> 00:05:49.348
rather than Puca intervention.

00:05:49.432 --> 00:05:50.975
The Puca remains
relatively the same

00:05:51.059 --> 00:05:53.186
in appearance and
action across Ireland

00:05:53.269 --> 00:05:54.562
and other parts of Europe,

00:05:54.645 --> 00:05:56.898
even as Christianity
becomes more prevalent.

00:05:56.981 --> 00:05:59.108
Sometimes characteristics
have important enough

00:05:59.192 --> 00:06:01.235
moral, cultural,
and social meaning

00:06:01.319 --> 00:06:03.279
that they need to
remain the same.

00:06:03.362 --> 00:06:05.573
Take the monster's
connection to The Wild Hunt,

00:06:05.656 --> 00:06:07.992
an archaic folklore
trope involving a divine

00:06:08.076 --> 00:06:09.619
or semi-divine figure

00:06:09.702 --> 00:06:12.747
leading a band of supernatural
entities and/or the dead

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:14.707
on a nighttime horseback hunt

00:06:14.791 --> 00:06:18.002
that causes general havoc
and fear amongst humans.

00:06:18.086 --> 00:06:19.295
The Wild Hunt is linked back

00:06:19.378 --> 00:06:21.214
to ancient cults of
fertility, death,

00:06:21.297 --> 00:06:23.883
ancestor worship, and magic.

00:06:23.966 --> 00:06:27.678
The Puca who forcefully takes
humans on death-defying rides

00:06:27.762 --> 00:06:28.763
could fit right
into this narrative,

00:06:28.846 --> 00:06:32.433
which would make
it very old indeed.

00:06:32.517 --> 00:06:34.602
The places the Puca appear
often bear that name

00:06:34.685 --> 00:06:38.314
and have ecological and
economic importance or value.

00:06:38.397 --> 00:06:40.733
There are marshes, rocks,
waterfalls, caverns, wells,

00:06:40.817 --> 00:06:43.277
and other spots
across Northern Europe

00:06:43.361 --> 00:06:46.781
with some variation of Puca
as part of their place names,

00:06:46.864 --> 00:06:48.407
designating biological habitats

00:06:48.491 --> 00:06:51.869
and resources that should
be left undisturbed by man.

00:06:51.953 --> 00:06:54.664
Places like Pollaphuca
and Puckstown.

00:06:54.747 --> 00:06:57.333
In Fayapooka in
Kerry County Ireland,

00:06:57.416 --> 00:07:00.336
it was said a Puca resided
undisturbed by mortals

00:07:00.419 --> 00:07:03.172
in a particular marsh
for over a century

00:07:03.256 --> 00:07:05.883
until a young man named
Tim Dorney inherited the land

00:07:05.967 --> 00:07:09.554
and decided to cut the marsh up
with drains and dishes.

00:07:09.637 --> 00:07:11.180
Driven from its home,

00:07:11.264 --> 00:07:14.934
the Puca becomes enraged and
begins harassing Tim's family.

00:07:15.017 --> 00:07:17.687
One night while surveying
his land for stray cattle,

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:19.856
Tim finds himself on
the back of a monster

00:07:19.939 --> 00:07:22.692
who is galloping
at a furious pace.

00:07:22.775 --> 00:07:26.654
Held on by an invisible force,
Tim is unable to dismount.

00:07:26.737 --> 00:07:28.823
The monster scrapes him against
stone walls, through bushes,

00:07:28.906 --> 00:07:32.535
and eventually almost
throws him off a cliff,

00:07:32.618 --> 00:07:35.246
deciding at the last second
to spare him,

00:07:35.329 --> 00:07:38.082
satisfied by
the man's immense fear.

00:07:38.166 --> 00:07:39.208
Tim is enraged by this encounter

00:07:39.292 --> 00:07:41.711
and swears revenge
on the creature.

00:07:41.794 --> 00:07:43.880
He takes to wearing
long-neck spurs

00:07:43.963 --> 00:07:45.923
and carries a weighted
whip with an iron grip.

00:07:46.007 --> 00:07:48.968
When the Puca takes him
for another wild ride,

00:07:49.051 --> 00:07:51.971
it suffers great pain
from these devices.

00:07:52.054 --> 00:07:55.558
These sharp things are enough
to deter the Puca for a time.

00:07:55.641 --> 00:07:58.436
Years pass until one night
when Tim is returning home

00:07:58.519 --> 00:08:01.564
from a late-night card game,
he comes across a small horse

00:08:01.647 --> 00:08:04.025
tied up next to
the bank of a river.

00:08:04.108 --> 00:08:06.360
The docile horse
wears a straw saddle

00:08:06.444 --> 00:08:09.322
with a white thorn
branch tucked inside.

00:08:09.405 --> 00:08:11.115
The white thorn plant
is said to be unlucky

00:08:11.199 --> 00:08:13.409
and can attract fairies.

00:08:13.492 --> 00:08:15.620
Tim recognizes this warning,
salutes the Puca,

00:08:15.703 --> 00:08:18.164
and continues to walk home.

00:08:18.247 --> 00:08:21.542
But this won't be the end of
his run-ins with the monster.

00:08:21.626 --> 00:08:23.211
The Puca avenges his injuries

00:08:23.294 --> 00:08:25.922
by driving Tim's
horses off a cliff,

00:08:26.005 --> 00:08:28.925
horses that were carrying
butter meant to pay Tim's rent.

00:08:29.008 --> 00:08:30.551
And without the butter,

00:08:30.635 --> 00:08:33.179
Tim loses the farm and the
Puca reigns victorious,

00:08:33.262 --> 00:08:36.432
having rid himself of
an inhospitable family.

00:08:36.515 --> 00:08:39.810
This story is a cautionary
tale against disrupting nature,

00:08:39.894 --> 00:08:41.979
but it also promotes
something else,

00:08:42.063 --> 00:08:44.106
a reverence for folklore.

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:46.984
Tim can keep his life and
his land for a longer period

00:08:47.068 --> 00:08:50.154
only because he recognizes
the white thorn branch

00:08:50.238 --> 00:08:51.656
as a warning,

00:08:51.739 --> 00:08:53.783
promoting the idea that
these so-called superstitions

00:08:53.866 --> 00:08:56.494
and legends protect
those who know them.

00:08:56.577 --> 00:08:57.536
Any crops left after sowen

00:08:57.620 --> 00:08:59.956
are considered
tainted by the Puca

00:09:00.039 --> 00:09:03.751
and in some places the monsters
left offerings as placation.

00:09:03.834 --> 00:09:06.420
This might be a way to
encourage resource management

00:09:06.504 --> 00:09:07.922
and ensure the harvest
is fully collected

00:09:08.005 --> 00:09:09.632
before it can spoil.

00:09:09.715 --> 00:09:12.093
Even when ridiculed,
the legends of the Puca

00:09:12.176 --> 00:09:15.304
are acknowledged as part of
national and cultural histories

00:09:15.388 --> 00:09:17.598
and over time, the
monster's integration

00:09:17.682 --> 00:09:19.809
into literature
and popular fiction

00:09:19.892 --> 00:09:23.312
solidifies its place
as a common social convention.

00:09:23.396 --> 00:09:25.690
Puck, the mischievous
shape-shifting narrator

00:09:25.773 --> 00:09:28.150
of Shakespeare's
"A Midsummer Night's Dream"

00:09:28.234 --> 00:09:32.196
clearly borrows its name and
nature from the Puca tradition.

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:34.156
Like the folkloric figure,
Shakespeare's Puck,

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:36.367
the merry wanderer of the night

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:39.245
is unbothered by the effects
of his actions on humans,

00:09:39.328 --> 00:09:41.706
which include misleading
night wanderers,

00:09:41.789 --> 00:09:44.583
crafting potions,
pouring drinks on people,

00:09:44.667 --> 00:09:47.461
and turning human heads
into donkey heads.

00:09:47.545 --> 00:09:50.631
But don't think the Puca's
influence ended centuries ago.

00:09:50.715 --> 00:09:51.716
It appears in the
guise of a black goat

00:09:51.799 --> 00:09:55.261
with long horns standing
on its hind legs

00:09:55.344 --> 00:09:58.014
in Thomas Crofton Croker's
"Fairy Legends and Traditions"

00:09:58.097 --> 00:10:01.225
in the 19th century,
a text whose great popularity

00:10:01.309 --> 00:10:04.854
undoubtedly introduced many
more people to the creature.

00:10:04.937 --> 00:10:05.938
In 20th century
plays and movies,

00:10:06.022 --> 00:10:08.899
the Puca's legend
influences characters,

00:10:08.983 --> 00:10:10.818
sometimes in a comedic way.

00:10:10.901 --> 00:10:12.653
In a 1950 film, "Harvey",

00:10:12.737 --> 00:10:16.449
a giant rabbit appears only
to main character Elwood.

00:10:16.532 --> 00:10:17.867
Harvey is mischievous,

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:20.286
pulling pranks and
causing confusion.

00:10:20.369 --> 00:10:22.997
About a decade later,
a more direct association

00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:26.417
turns up in the movie "Darby
O'Gill and the Little People".

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:28.502
An Irish king owns
a mischievous horse

00:10:28.586 --> 00:10:32.423
that can change color,
disappear, and is named Pooka.

00:10:32.506 --> 00:10:34.633
In the 21st century,
we get more modern

00:10:34.717 --> 00:10:36.969
and metaphorical versions
of the Puca.

00:10:37.053 --> 00:10:39.221
Fans and some
scholars, I'm both,

00:10:39.305 --> 00:10:41.891
of the 2001
cult classic "Donnie Darko"

00:10:41.974 --> 00:10:44.185
believe a Puca is at its core,

00:10:44.268 --> 00:10:45.978
one who leans into
the more malevolent

00:10:46.062 --> 00:10:48.105
mischievousness of the monster.

00:10:48.189 --> 00:10:49.357
Then there's Pooka

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:51.942
from the "Into the Dark"
horror anthology.

00:10:52.026 --> 00:10:53.361
This might be the most unusual

00:10:53.444 --> 00:10:56.489
and unnerving
interpretation of the Puca,

00:10:56.572 --> 00:10:58.574
a toy that records
what its owner says

00:10:58.657 --> 00:11:00.659
in either naughty or nice mode.

00:11:00.743 --> 00:11:02.703
When an actor's hired
to wear the promotional

00:11:02.787 --> 00:11:03.788
first suit version
of the character,

00:11:03.871 --> 00:11:06.457
an interesting take
on the monster's

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:08.000
shape-shifting abilities,

00:11:08.084 --> 00:11:11.629
it quickly consumes him,
taking over his personality.

00:11:11.712 --> 00:11:13.964
Stories of the Puca
continue to circulate today,

00:11:14.048 --> 00:11:16.634
both in oral tradition
and popular culture.

00:11:16.717 --> 00:11:18.969
Whatever form the
shape-shifting creature takes,

00:11:19.053 --> 00:11:22.723
it always manages to disrupt
the lives of those around it,

00:11:22.807 --> 00:11:24.350
for better or worse.

00:11:24.433 --> 00:11:27.561
The very malleability of the
Puca's form helps it endure.

00:11:27.645 --> 00:11:28.813
Who knows what shape
it will take next?

00:11:30.481 --> 00:11:32.024
Am I on the right mark?

00:11:32.108 --> 00:11:33.818
(Director)
Yep, you're perfect.

00:11:33.901 --> 00:11:35.277
-[claps]
-Sorry.

00:11:36.570 --> 00:11:38.906
It was literally right
in front of my face.

00:11:38.989 --> 00:11:40.366
Okay.

00:11:40.449 --> 00:11:42.284
Nature indicated with
completely black coloring

00:11:42.368 --> 00:11:44.829
or animal features like
ears, horns, or a tail.

00:11:44.912 --> 00:11:46.747
[pair laughing]

